1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:02,861
Every roof loses sun to something.

2
00:00:03,500 --> 00:00:04,321
A stair room.

3
00:00:04,321 --> 00:00:05,143
A water tank.

4
00:00:05,143 --> 00:00:06,407
The floor next door.

5
00:00:06,407 --> 00:00:06,849
A tree.

6
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:10,621
And how much it loses decides whether solar is worth it.

7
00:00:15,800 --> 00:00:18,722
Before going solar, a household needs four answers.

8
00:00:19,200 --> 00:00:20,925
Where can panels actually fit?

9
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:22,936
What shades them, and when?

10
00:00:23,600 --> 00:00:24,865
How much will they make?

11
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:27,911
And what will it really cost, after the subsidy?

12
00:00:29,400 --> 00:00:35,459
Today, those answers come from a site visit and a round of quotes, and the household can't check any of them.

13
00:00:40,700 --> 00:00:43,711
homesol answers all four, from one tap on a map.

14
00:00:46,300 --> 00:00:50,424
Here's a home in Chandigarh, in Google's own photo of the street.

15
00:00:50,424 --> 00:00:51,756
One tap, on its roof.

16
00:00:53,800 --> 00:01:04,562
It measures the roof from Google's 3D survey: flat, two hundred and twenty-five square metres, about two floors up, and it finds everything around that could cast a shadow.

17
00:01:04,562 --> 00:01:06,502
That's geometry, not guesswork.

18
00:01:07,300 --> 00:01:14,439
Then Claude, on Amazon Bedrock, names what was measured: a large cluster of trees to the south-west, a stair room.

19
00:01:14,439 --> 00:01:15,629
It can name things.

20
00:01:15,629 --> 00:01:17,508
It can't change a measurement.

21
00:01:19,700 --> 00:01:28,223
Next, it runs the sun across the year, every half hour, over those heights, and charges each hour of lost sunlight to whatever blocked it.

22
00:01:35,200 --> 00:01:37,311
The trees take fifty-six percent.

23
00:01:37,311 --> 00:01:42,941
On the shortest day, the panel spots are clear of shadow only from about ten until noon.

24
00:01:44,200 --> 00:01:46,199
Panels go only where the sun is good.

25
00:01:46,199 --> 00:01:50,899
There's room for twenty-eight, in rows spaced so they don't shade each other in winter.

26
00:01:51,400 --> 00:01:56,859
Say this household uses three hundred and fifty units a month, about nineteen hundred rupees.

27
00:01:58,900 --> 00:02:03,558
The plan: six panels, three point three kilowatts, covering all of it.

28
00:02:03,558 --> 00:02:12,275
After the seventy-eight thousand rupee PM Surya Ghar subsidy, it costs one lakh twenty thousand, and pays back in about five years.

29
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:19,015
And every figure says where it came from: the survey's date, the subsidy rules, the household's own rate.

30
00:02:19,600 --> 00:02:22,222
Now, a what-if: what about five kilowatts?

31
00:02:22,222 --> 00:02:28,154
A Strands agent on Claude answers from this plan, using the same finance code as the dashboard.

32
00:02:28,600 --> 00:02:34,826
The bigger system would take about nine years to pay back, not five, because the extra units would earn nothing.

33
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:46,432
Across eighty-one test questions, seven hundred and eighty-nine of seven hundred and ninety automatic checks passed, and every money figure traced back to the plan's own numbers.

34
00:02:46,800 --> 00:02:47,287
One tap.

35
00:02:47,287 --> 00:02:53,259
A measured roof, a year of shadows, a panel plan and an honest price, before the first site visit.

36
00:02:54,600 --> 00:02:55,631
That's homesol.
